    "updated_at": "2023-04-15T04:29:42Z",
    "body": "There have been lots of improvements to git for large repos recently:\r\nhttps://github.blog/2022-06-29-improve-git-monorepo-performance-with-a-file-system-monitor/\r\n\r\nTo summarise, git has a built in file system monitor daemon, which `git status` communicates with using IPC to retrieve a list of changed files, instead of having to read the actual files on disk. The article claims it improves other commands like `diff`, `add .`, `restore` and `checkout` as well.\r\n\r\nIf you're on Mac or Windows, you can try these config options to speed up `git status` (ensure `git --version` is >= 2.37.0):\r\n```\r\ngit config core.fsmonitor true\r\ngit config core.untrackedCache true\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere is support coming for the built-in fsmonitor on linux, see https://github.com/git/git/pull/1352. You can still use watchman with the git hook described in the article on linux (I think).\r\n\r\nAnother thing to try is `git config feature.manyFiles true`\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
